                        <foaf:surname>Schubotz</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenname>Ricarda Ines</foaf:givenname>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>

        <dcterms:isPartOf>
            <bib:Journal>
                <dc:title>Psychological Research</dc:title>
                <prism:volume>71</prism:volume>
                <prism:number>1</prism:number>
                <dc:identifier>ISSN 0340-0727, 1430-2772</dc:identifier>
            </bib:Journal>
        </dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dc:title>Intentional control of attention: action planning primes action-related stimulus dimensions</dc:title>
        <bib:pages>22--29</bib:pages>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1007/s00426-005-0033-3</dc:identifier>
        <dc:identifier><dcterms:URI><rdf:value>http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00426-005-0033-3</rdf:value></dcterms:URI></dc:identifier>
        <dc:date>2007-01-11</dc:date>
        <z:language>english</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Intentional control of attention</z:shortTitle>
        <dc:rights>http://www.springer.com/tdm</dc:rights>
        <dc:description>not read; used in discussion for RT;What they tested
They investigated whether planning an action influences attention and perception, even before the action is actually executed. Specifically, participants prepared for either a reaching action or a grasping action. While they kept that action plan active (before doing it), they were presented with stimulus events that varied in perceptual features (like location or size). The question was:

Does preparing to move in a particular way make the brain process certain stimulus dimensions more effectively?


What they found


While participants were preparing a reaching action, they detected changes in location-based stimuli more quickly.


When preparing a grasping action, they detected changes in size-based stimuli more quickly.


This effect happened before actual movement execution, suggesting that merely planning the action biased perception toward action-related perceptual dimensions.



What it means (in simple terms)
Planning an action doesn’t just prepare the muscles — it also changes how your brain allocates attention to the world. If you plan to reach for something, you become better at noticing where things are. If you plan to grasp something, you become better at noticing what size things are. This suggests that action plans and attention/perception share processing resources.

Why it matters for your work
Although this study is not about sense of agency per se, it shows a key principle that is relevant for agency research:

Action preparation influences cognitive and perceptual processes even before action execution.

This supports the idea that in your experiment, active movement (even just planning it) can change how participants process subsequent stimuli and make judgments — including how they experience agency. This fits well with theoretical frameworks like the Theory of Event Coding (TEC) and intentional weighting mechanisms, which argue that action and perception are tightly coupled at early stages of processing.</dc:description>
        <dc:subject>MT_soa-motor</dc:subject>
    </bib:Article>

